  {
    "episode_id": 97130,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97130.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Sure, I'm the daughter and the granddaughter of true small business entrepreneurs. And I began working, believe it or not, at my parents' small business at the age of 12. And they owned small retail carpet stores. One was called Carpet Time. The other was called The Last Yard. And it was there that I I really learned the value of a dollar, a good, strong Midwestern work ethic, and certainly how to treat customers. Customer was king. And my father would just always say he'd pound a desk, a tabletop, a counter, whatever it was. And he'd say, and Louise, all I want is to get the government off my back and out of my way. And I love that. I want to provide the goods and services to the community. I want to take care of the employees and provide for my family. And I'll never forget those words. They absolutely are a part of my DNA. And I have to say that that has informed much of my work going forward. I ran for office, frankly, to serve a cause greater than one self. This is not a job, it's a calling for",
