  {
    "episode_id": 97130,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97130.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "And my bill increases your average retail investor's exposure to private markets. And I think it's going to have the protections that we need. And there's this sense that wealth, I don't believe wealth is just not a proxy for sophistication. And we can't leave these opportunities out there just for the wealthiest Americans, they got to give them the access and whether we expand the definition of accredited investor to include those with certain licenses or education or job experiences or we remove unnecessary restrictions on a close end funds ability to invest in private funds as my bill does. We've shown that there are many policy options worth exploring that can increase access to investment opportunities without compromising true investor protection. So I'm excited. I'm excited about pulling all these bills together in capital formation. It's obviously a key focus because it's what we came out of the gate with. when we constituted the new 118th Congress Financial Services Committee, and to get back, finish up that fourth hearing, move to markup, and then most importantly, move to these bills to the floor is key. And as I said, a good number of them, we're still working at some of the of the Kings, but a good number of them, Marsha, are bipartisan. So I think We'll have an ability to pass these. I see this whole package is kind of a jobs 2.0 package. And I believe that our country is built on small businesses and entrepreneurs. And we have to make sure they have the kind of of capital investment available to them to grow and we have to make sure that your everyday retail investor who I believe is much more sophisticated than we give folks credit for should have an opportunity to participate in some of this great new startups and small businesses and these fantastic opportunities that we're seeing come from so many of our innovation hubs across the country and are certainly key in my hometown of St. Louis and in places like Little Rock. And as you said, in Pennsylvania and other places, they're really key to letting people pursue the American dream. So I'm excited to be moving this package and we'll be doing so very quickly. And I thank you for all your great time and for your podcast and your interest. You've been a great resource for us, helping us find good witnesses like Mr. Vasquez and others that really understand the early stage private company and how important this kind of angel capital is in growing. So I thank you for that.",
